  "problem": "${lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}\u2061(t) \\\\  & =2{csc}^{2}\u2061(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}\u2061(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942110/assets/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}\u2061(2\\pi ) & ={lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}\u2061(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $2$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$",
